<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clan Douglas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Black Douglases, Earls of Douglas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clan Douglas was a Scottish noble family whose fortune was made by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-douglas-governor">Sir James Douglas</a>, the companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a>. The earls of Douglas, created in 1358, became the most powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> in late medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, dominating the Borders and often the royal government. James II broke the main line, killing the eighth earl in 1452 and defeating the family in 1455, while the Red Douglas earls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angus">Angus</a> remained prominent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clan-douglas.jpg" alt="Clan Douglas" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Celtus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clan_member_crest_badge_-_Clan_Douglas.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family took its name from the village of Douglas in Lanarkshire, a name modern writers derive from the Gaelic dubh, dark or black, and glas, stream. A tradition that the first chief was Sholto Douglas, who helped a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> win a battle in 767, is unsubstantiated and likely pseudohistory. The first certain record of the name is William Douglas, Lord of Douglas in 1179, who witnessed a charter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> to the monks of Kelso. Two sons of his grandson fought against the Norsemen at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-largs">Battle of Largs</a> in 1263.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Wars of Scottish Independence Sir William Douglas le Hardi, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berwick-upon-tweed">Berwick-upon-Tweed</a>, was captured when the English besieged the town, later joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wallace">William Wallace</a>, and died a prisoner in the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1298. His son James, the Good Sir James, shared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a>'s defeats at Methven and Dalrigh in 1306, then waged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare the Scots called secret war, recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxburgh-castle">Roxburgh Castle</a> in 1313 and fought at Bannockburn in 1314.</p><p>Archibald Douglas, 3rd Earl of Douglas, defended Edinburgh Castle against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-england">Henry IV of England</a> in 1400. His son, the 4th Earl, was taken prisoner at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a> in 1403, became one of the regents for the young James I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, led 10,000 Scots to the aid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vii-of-france">Charles VII of France</a> in 1423, was made Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a> and was killed at the Battle of Verneuil on 17 August 1424. Of the Red Douglases, two sons of Archibald Douglas, 5th Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angus">Angus</a>, fell at Flodden in 1513, and the 6th Earl married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-tudor">Margaret Tudor</a> and won the Battle of Ancrum Moor in 1545.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1660 William Douglas, brother of the second Marquess of Douglas, became Duke of Hamilton through marriage, and the titles of Marquess of Douglas and Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angus">Angus</a> later passed to the Dukes of Hamilton. Because their surname is Douglas-Hamilton, the Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> does not recognise them as chiefs, and the clan has no recognised chief. A Swedish branch descends from Robert Douglas, who went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1627 as an officer in the Thirty Years' War, was made a count in 1654 and built Stjärnorp Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostergotland">Östergötland</a> from 1655.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Clan Douglas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Arkinholm near Langholm, a small action of only a few hundred troops decided the civil war between King James II and the <b>Black Douglases</b>, the most powerful noble family in Scotland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-1">1 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
